New material on the table, Trav’s homeworld and pre-academy life, his father six years dead, Yssa doing freelance work groundside, childhood split between shipboard life and the drums. Three shapes: it replaces the drafted Ch. 1, it runs as a prologue, or it folds in as a flashback inside the existing arrival scene. This touches already-delivered prose. **Lane:** Author. Structural. **Update, new:** Ch.12 (“The Choice”) now carries a dramatized home-visit scene as part of the new weekend-pattern lock (see Qiraki_Book1_Chapter_Outline.md), a second real candidate for this homeworld material if it doesn’t end up at the opening. Doesn’t resolve this decision, just widens the option space, this is still yours to call.

### DEC-03 — The socket’s payoff shape

LOCKS locks it to Book 7 and forbids any mention across Books 2 through 6, because the payoff depends on the reader having half-forgotten it. AUDIT §1.2 predates that lock and still lists three open shapes. AUDIT is stale here. **Status:** effectively resolved by LOCKS. Confirm and close. **Lane:** Author, one word.

### DEC-04 — Krethis’s resentment, six books unexplained

Opened B2.CH03, recurs in four more chapters, never explained. The two-part trope rule requires in-book resolution and a downstream echo. This has neither. **Two defensible answers:** it resolves in Book 7, or it is deliberately a permanent condition. The second is a real choice and works, but it has to be written down, because an unresolved thread reads as forgotten unless the documents say otherwise. **Lane:** Author.

### DEC-33 — Ironmind’s placement — RULED 2026-09-06

**RULED:** not in the one-go spend. Reqa prices Ironmind into the budget in the military era’s progression chapter (C2a) and Trav deletes the line. The budget genuinely does not hold it alongside the Arcblade, which is what keeps the deletion from reading as stubbornness, and he takes it off because it is not a weapon and he has never connected the pain to a line on a sheet, which is the same machinery that misses Ilyen for six books. **The purchase lands eight books later at Ilyen’s near-loss, Book 14,** using the debrief beat already locked there: she says two numbers instead of one, the envelope and then seventeen million, flat, and does not say what the second is for. He buys it off the page. **No new station added to Book 14 and no new chapter anywhere.** Executed in Military Era Outline v5, Era 2 Outline v4, CAST v24. **Lane:** Author, exercised. **Closed.**

### DEC-05 — The symbiosis pain has no Book 6 beat — RESOLVED 2026-09-06 by DEC-33

Begins B3.CH08, worsens through Books 4 and 5, then Book 6 is silent. PTS locks Ironmind as the eventual answer and places it well after the roughest years, so silence may be correct. It read as dropped rather than as decided. The three options on the table were that it plateaus, that it fades with maturity, or that he is simply used to it.

**None of those is the answer and the silence was the thread.** Nobody names it for eleven books. The first time it becomes nameable at all is a line on a purchase order in the military era that gets crossed out without being looked at. The person who eventually says it out loud is Ilyen, whose entire locked function is noticing precisely and being missed by him, and who by then has his numbers in front of her every mission because she is his Munti. Book 6’s silence is the middle of that, not a gap in it. **Follows from DEC-33 rather than being a separate ruling. Lane:** Author, exercised. **Closed.**

### DEC-07 — Carabil’s true spacefaring history, reveal or background fact

New this session: Carabil were already spacefaring before human contact, the common “uplift” framing in COE institutional material is false. Whether this stays a fact the documents know and the prose never dwells on, or gets an actual reveal beat somewhere (parallel mechanism to the buried Hiopi/Jhubi conquest chain, or a character’s private correction of someone else’s wrong assumption), is genuinely open. See Qiraki_Physical_Description_Bank.md and Qiraki_Concept.md’s Carabil entry for the new material itself. **Lane:** Author.

### DEC-08 — Act 2 has no clean weekend-chapter slot, add one or leave it thin

New this session, while building out the stay-weekend device: Ch.30 and Ch.46 converted cleanly into weekend chapters, zero renumbering, both were already tagged “ordinary” with no locked propaganda requirement competing for the slot. Act 2 (Apr-Jul) has no equivalent free chapter, every one of its 10 chapters already carries a locked beat or a padded secondary beat, and the two that are tonally closest to available (Ch.20 “June,” Ch.13 “Who’s Good At What”) are either propaganda-locked (June hosts a Year 1 Bloom-curriculum fragment, can’t lose its opener) or already doing real character-differentiation work not worth diluting. **Real cost of adding a genuinely new chapter here, checked rather than assumed:** at least seven other files cross-reference specific Book 1 chapter numbers by digit (Ch.1, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, 24, across Qiraki_Character_Sheets.md, Qiraki_Path_Audit_Books1-6.md, Qiraki_Session_Locks_Addendum.md, Qiraki_Chapter_Build_Protocol.md, Qiraki_Outline_Update_Log.md, Qiraki_Concept.md, and Book 2’s own outline), and inserting a chapter anywhere before Ch.24 shifts every one of them. Recommendation: leave Act 2 without a dedicated weekend chapter for now, real weekends there stay implied rather than shown, same as every other month that doesn’t get one. If a third one is genuinely wanted, it’s a dedicated renumbering pass, not a same-session add. **Lane:** Author.

### DEC-09 — Does the Survey half-know about the Bloom’s extragalactic origin before the withdrawal

From Qiraki_Political_Web.md POL-01. Currently locked that the Coalition only learns the Bloom is extragalactic after the withdrawal. Costs nothing to establish and never needs its own scene: an External Survey analysis that was correct, underfunded, filed, and never escalated, Wohlstetter’s signal-to-noise problem rather than a conspiracy. Gives the sixty-year failure a paper trail nobody hid. POL recommends taking it, doesn’t take it. **Lane:** Author.

### DEC-10 — Which pillar breaks first in the civil war

From Qiraki_Political_Web.md POL-02. The Ledger’s early defection is already locked, but the Ledger is a faction, not a pillar. Whether the Staff (the military pillar) fractures, defects wholesale, or holds to the last is unresolved, and it determines whether Trav’s war reads as rebellion or mutiny. **Lane:** Author, and probably the single largest open call in the political era.

### DEC-11 — Is there a head of state above the Quorum

From Qiraki_Political_Web.md POL-03. Built in POL as four seats, no fifth chair, deliberately, so the four-hub endgame campaign is a literal necessity rather than a symbolic one. A single figure above the Quorum is an equally workable structure but changes the endgame’s shape. **Lane:** Author.

### DEC-12 — Sovereign-grade longevity against Quorum seat turnover, endgame shape

